# =============================================================================
# device-summa-recollige — S2-5 ordinary two-kernel fixture (gpu-training-lowering)
# =============================================================================
#
# What this proves:
#   • Two dependent ordinary collection kernels — a tree reduction (`collige`,
#     the proven `summa` recipe over a tf32[1024] buffer producing 4
#     per-workgroup partials) and a second tree reduction (`recollige`, the
#     same recipe over the tf32[4] intermediate) — with a device-resident
#     intermediate (`medius`, tf32[4]) shared by data-flow identity: kernel 2
#     consumes exactly what kernel 1 wrote on the device; nothing round-trips
#     through the CPU between kernels (no intermediate CPU recomputation, no
#     undeclared readback).
#   • The ordinary command on both backends:
#
#       faber run --backend metal <package>    # Apple M5 Max (burgus)
#       faber run --backend cuda  <package>    # NVIDIA RTX 5070 (pharos)
#
#   • The constructor unifies the cross-kernel buffer identity (S2-5):
#     `medius` is ONE BufferId (InOut role, PerStep lifetime) across both
#     kernels — the shared intermediate is identity-unified by name+shape,
#     never aliased (N2.7 no-aliasing posture).
#
# Kernel 2 is a reduction rather than an elementwise kernel because the
# Metal emitter's collection-op surface deliberately stops at Sum/Mean and
# matmul (elementwise TensorAdd "waits for its own recipe" — D-W6-A1 U3);
# the spec sanctions "reduction + elementwise, or two reductions" (N2.6).
#
# The host input values live in faber.toml `[device] inputs` (pinned with the
# oracle); the CPU reference is pinned in oracle/ (numeric-policy v1.0.0,
# reduction-sum row atol=1e-6/rtol=1e-6).
#
# See also: nucleum, tensor, cuda, device-summa
# =============================================================================

# Kernel 1 — reduction: sum the whole tf32[1024] tensor; the recipe writes
# one per-workgroup partial per output slot (4 workgroups × 256 lanes → 4
# partials). `id` is the workgroup index.
@ nucleum
functio collige(tf32[1024] a, tf32[4] medius, u32 id) → vacuum {
    fixum f32 total ← a.summa()
    medius[id] ← total
}

# Kernel 2 — reduction: sum the tf32[4] intermediate (the device-resident
# partials written by kernel 1) into the final tf32[1] result. 4 lanes →
# 1 workgroup → 1 partial.
@ nucleum
functio recollige(tf32[4] medius, tf32[1] result, u32 id) → vacuum {
    fixum f32 total ← medius.summa()
    result[id] ← total
}
